    "post_id": 34960417,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 3580226,
    "poster_id": 908074,
    "post_rendered": "In a scalene triangle <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\"  width=\"42\" height=\"13\" >,</span> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" > is the point of tangency of the incircle with the side <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\"  width=\"28\" height=\"12\" >.</span> Points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/8/448acda25892600fa0730fc77a20586aec4e3f33.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T_B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"20\" height=\"15\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/b/0fbb42463d5e93f8c8acb59d3c1b42e0e3432c04.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T_C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"20\" height=\"15\" > are the intersections of the angle bisectors of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/a/a3ad57710b6810476e6017ea2c2cc6d41219c218.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"57\" height=\"14\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/2/0728bebb477a2636300c56851b6a6f9e382c1b83.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ACB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"57\" height=\"14\" > with the circumcircle of <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\"  width=\"42\" height=\"13\" >,</span> respectively. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/a/21a033e6eebef58c63d09041d5b868891a5052cd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X_B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"25\" height=\"15\" > be the antipodal point of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" > in the circumcircle of <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/e/6ce2cd123db24a7b8373999aea7a98927e3b3c81.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ACD$\"  width=\"43\" height=\"13\" >,</span> and let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/6/6d6593f6233f4936e5cb55f35b7febc53af525b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X_C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"25\" height=\"15\" > be the antipodal point of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" > in the circumcircle of <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/a/a6adfd3ad27a2dddeb0af7131d73c33c86991e93.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" >.</span><br>\nProve that triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/7/d47d927982894165140042b75b61154407a781fc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B T_C X_C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"65\" height=\"17\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/b/b9b1967b3d7369f35be6ffe337fb5bd0450c3b44.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C T_B X_B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"65\" height=\"17\" > are similar.<br>\nProposed by Sobhan Aram",
    "post_canonical": "In a scalene triangle $ABC$, $D$ is the point of tangency of the incircle with the side $BC$. Points $T_B$ and $T_C$ are the intersections of the angle bisectors of $\\angle ABC$ and $\\angle ACB$ with the circumcircle of $ABC$, respectively. Let $X_B$ be the antipodal point of $A$ in the circumcircle of $ACD$, and let $X_C$ be the antipodal point of $A$ in the circumcircle of $ABD$.\nProve that triangles $B T_C X_C$ and $C T_B X_B$ are similar.\nProposed by Sobhan Aram",
