  {
   "item_id": 34960194,
   "item_text": "10",
   "item_forum_id": 0,
   "item_type": "post",
   "in_feed": "0",
   "is_watched": "0",
   "item_score": "0",
   "id": "41293294",
   "item_level": "3",
   "post_data": {
    "post_id": 34960194,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 3580181,
    "poster_id": 933348,
    "post_rendered": "A number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/d/a6d71630f25bda277a7c733cb394971f9ac5eef5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"\\( n \\)\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" > is called lucky if it has at least two distinct prime divisors and can be written in the form:<br>\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/4/744a5ccaeb9476ebd7d999c395762cb6e99a7a71.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\nn = p_1^{\\alpha_1} + \\cdots + p_k^{\\alpha_k}\n\\]\"  width=\"149\" height=\"18\" >where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/5/ac5827b3c8b378eea0a40c0c9d01b09f168c7f06.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"\\( p_1, \\dots, p_k \\)\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"76\" height=\"13\" > are distinct prime numbers that divide <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/d/a6d71630f25bda277a7c733cb394971f9ac5eef5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"\\( n \\)\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" >.</span> (Note: it is possible that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/d/a6d71630f25bda277a7c733cb394971f9ac5eef5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"\\( n \\)\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" > has other prime divisors not among <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/5/ac5827b3c8b378eea0a40c0c9d01b09f168c7f06.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"\\( p_1, \\dots, p_k \\)\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"76\" height=\"13\" >.</span>) Prove that for every prime number <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/3/d432d346fc283bc4a1a8e6623d8454f9d35801a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"\\( p \\)\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" >,</span> there exists a lucky number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/d/a6d71630f25bda277a7c733cb394971f9ac5eef5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"\\( n \\)\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" > such that <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/8/188228cd4f08a83f909dd2b3915f592c66a7ecfd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"\\( p \\mid n \\)\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"37\" height=\"20\" >.</span><br>\n<br>\n<i>Proposed by Navid Safaei</i>",
    "post_canonical": "A number \\( n \\) is called lucky if it has at least two distinct prime divisors and can be written in the form:\n\\[\nn = p_1^{\\alpha_1} + \\cdots + p_k^{\\alpha_k}\n\\]\nwhere \\( p_1, \\dots, p_k \\) are distinct prime numbers that divide \\( n \\). (Note: it is possible that \\( n \\) has other prime divisors not among \\( p_1, \\dots, p_k \\).) Prove that for every prime number \\( p \\), there exists a lucky number \\( n \\) such that \\( p \\mid n \\).\n\n[i]Proposed by Navid Safaei[/i]",
