    "post_rendered": "In a scalene triangle <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\"  width=\"42\" height=\"13\" >,</span> points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/5/ce58e4af225c93d08606c26554caaa5ae32edeba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" > lie on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\"  width=\"27\" height=\"13\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\"  width=\"28\" height=\"12\" > respectively such that <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/6/346bbd53153971a4479fe0ece58cc779f1a40b8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC \\perp XY$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"86\" height=\"14\" >.</span> Points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/a/3ead47fb9fb4a4c273feee398f72ff2a09702b84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" > are the reflections of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/5/ce58e4af225c93d08606c26554caaa5ae32edeba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" > with respect to the midpoints of sides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\"  width=\"28\" height=\"12\" > and <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\"  width=\"27\" height=\"13\" >,</span> respectively. Point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" > lies on segment <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/d/83dbf49889eebbe3dd7d773c6d4d4b1bfd3533bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ZT$\"  width=\"26\" height=\"12\" > such that the circumcenter of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/4/db4ca2bdeb3334d0d3f22bbb824b2c9225351265.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$XZP$\"  width=\"44\" height=\"12\" > coincides with the circumcenter of triangle <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\"  width=\"42\" height=\"13\" >.</span><br>\nProve that the nine-point circle of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\"  width=\"42\" height=\"13\" > passes through the midpoint of segment <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/255140b38e3c950b5b5927eb6b6a72245cfaa7be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$XP$\"  width=\"30\" height=\"12\" >.</span><br>\nProposed by Amirmahdi Mohseni",
    "post_canonical": "In a scalene triangle $ABC$, points $Y$ and $X$ lie on $AC$ and $BC$ respectively such that $BC \\perp XY$. Points $Z$ and $T$ are the reflections of $X$ and $Y$ with respect to the midpoints of sides $BC$ and $AC$, respectively. Point $P$ lies on segment $ZT$ such that the circumcenter of triangle $XZP$ coincides with the circumcenter of triangle $ABC$.\nProve that the nine-point circle of triangle $ABC$ passes through the midpoint of segment $XP$.\nProposed by Amirmahdi Mohseni",
